---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../lib/site';
import { STATES } from '../data/complianceMap';
import { CASE_QUOTES as Q } from '../data/grievanceFile';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="For County Risk Pools and Insurers: Pest Control Exposure in Member Jails"
  description="How a pest grievance in a jail becomes a claim, the three questions to ask a member jail, and the free tools on the Corrections Pest Reference that answer them: the model pest control policy, the grievance-to-work-order loop, the Vermin Grievance File, and the Compliance Map."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'For Risk Pools', url: `${SITE_URL}/risk-pools/` }]}
>
  <style>
    .lede { font-size: 1.05rem; max-width: 46rem; }
    .q { border: 1px solid var(--color-rule); border-radius: 6px; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1rem 0; max-width: 46rem; }
    .q h3 { margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-size: 1.05rem; }
    .q p { margin: 0.3rem 0; }
    .q .tools { font-size: 0.92rem; }
    .cite { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--color-text-meta); }
    section { max-width: 46rem; }
  </style>

  <h1>For County Risk Pools and Insurers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A pest grievance in a jail can become a claim. This page puts the reference's free tools in the order a risk manager or
    loss-control consultant would use them with a member jail: where the exposure is, three questions to ask, and what the
    member's own state asks for.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Where the exposure is</h2>
    <p>
      When a person held in a jail claims that cockroaches, mice, bed bugs, or lice violated the Constitution, the claim turns on
      deliberate indifference. An official is liable only if the official "{Q.farmerKnows.text}" <span class="cite">({Q.farmerKnows.cite})</span>.
      And officials who knew "{Q.farmerReasonable.text}" <span class="cite">({Q.farmerReasonable.cite})</span>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both halves are proved with paper. Knowledge is usually proved with the jail's own grievance, kite, or sick-call record. A
      reasonable response has to be proved with records too, and a spray record alone may not be enough: "{Q.antonelliInsufficient.text}"
      <span class="cite">({Q.antonelliInsufficient.cite})</span>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/vermin-grievance-file/">Vermin Grievance File</a> walks through both halves, question by question.
      It is a records worksheet, not legal advice.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Three questions to ask a member jail</h2>

    <div class="q">
      <h3>1. Does the written pest control policy set a frequency and a provider, and does the service agreement deliver them?</h3>
      <p>
        Many standards ask for a written policy and stop there, so the policy is where the frequency gets set. Some custody
        manuals set it already: Lexipol's Policy 805 calls for a licensed pest control professional to inspect at least monthly.
        A quarterly or on-call contract under that policy is a gap in the jail's own file.
      </p>
      <p class="tools">
        Tools: <a href="/tools/pest-control-policy/">Model Pest Control Policy</a> ·
        <a href="/authorities/lexipol-custody-manual-policy-805-vermin-pest-control/">Lexipol Policy 805</a> ·
        <a href="/tools/scope-of-work/">Model Scope of Work</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="q">
      <h3>2. Does every pest report become a work order, with a follow-up check and an answer?</h3>
      <p>
        The loop usually breaks in the same places: the complaint never becomes a work order, the service visit records no
        location, nobody checks whether the treatment worked, or the grievance is answered before the work was done.
      </p>
      <p class="tools">Tool: <a href="/tools/grievance-loop/">Grievance-to-Work-Order Loop</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="q">
      <h3>3. For the last pest grievance, can the file answer the eight questions?</h3>
      <p>
        When did the jail learn of it, who received it, what was done and when, by whom and under what license, what was found
        and applied, was the person moved, did someone check whether it worked, and what was the grievant told.
      </p>
      <p class="tools">Tool: <a href="/tools/vermin-grievance-file/">The Vermin Grievance File</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the member's state asks for</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/compliance-map/">Pest Compliance Map</a> lists, for a facility profile in any of {STATES.length} states,
      every authority on this reference that reaches it, what each requires, and the record that shows it was met. Each state
      has a ready-made county jail checklist PDF. The <a href="/state-jail-standards/">{STATES.length}-state comparison</a> puts
      each state's jail pest provision side by side, with its legal force.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Sharing it with members</h2>
    <p>
      Every tool is free, with no login. Link to any page in a member bulletin or loss-control newsletter, or put it on screen
      in a training session. For a member training session or webinar led by the author, see <a href="/consulting/">Consulting</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
